Every package must do useful work and stay easy to add and remove.
This is the house rule behind the "clean playground" name.
A cartridge is a package for one job, with the resources that job needs.
It must stay self-contained and easy to delete.

Everything built here must do real work. `VISION.md` rejects toy examples that exist only to demonstrate a pattern.

rat-stack is a reference that other projects copy and maintain as source.

Its example CLI inspects real files. Its [database cartridge](/systems/database) owns a `RunLog` service and Layers for two database implementations.

A service is a named interface for a job. A Layer describes how to build services and supply their dependencies.
The website's search and read [capabilities](/systems/capabilities), named actions with shared contracts and handlers, serve the public repo. Each piece does real work.

The clean-playground test asks what remains when one package is removed. Removal must leave other packages' import paths unchanged, keep resource connections contained, and pass all required checks, tests, and builds.

`VISION.md` describes the cartridge test. `AGENTS.md` assigns each package one job and sets the required checks.

[Analytics](/systems/analytics) supplies another working package, with an explicit removal checklist. [Interest signup](/systems/interest) keeps delivery in an [adapter package](/lore/hexagonal-architecture) outside core. One-line infrastructure removal remains unproven for that adapter.

See [cartridges](/lore/cartridges) for the infrastructure example and [the fence](/lore/the-fence), the compiler checks, lint rules, tests, and hooks that enforce these rules.
